# Release signing settings for scripts/bundle.sh, which reads a .env file next to Cargo.toml.
# Copy this file to .env and set what you use; .env is ignored by git. The app itself reads
# no environment variables and needs no keys: it makes no network connections.
#
# No secret belongs in this file. notarytool keeps the Apple ID credentials in the keychain
# (see EQ_NOTARY_PROFILE), and CI takes its certificate and passwords from GitHub secrets.

# The codesign identity. "-" signs ad hoc, which is fine for your own builds, but Gatekeeper
# warns everyone who downloads one. To ship, use your Developer ID Application certificate,
# as listed by `security find-identity -v -p codesigning`:
# EQ_SIGN_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: Your Name (TEAMID1234)"
EQ_SIGN_IDENTITY=-

# A notarytool keychain profile, created once with
#   xcrun notarytool store-credentials eq-notary --apple-id you@example.com --team-id TEAMID1234
# With it, `./scripts/bundle.sh --zip` notarizes the zip and staples the ticket to the app.
# Notarization needs a Developer ID identity above.
# EQ_NOTARY_PROFILE=eq-notary

# The keychain that holds the profile, if it is not your login keychain.
# EQ_NOTARY_KEYCHAIN=

# 0 signs without the hardened runtime. Not recommended: the runtime keeps other code out of
# EQ, which may record system audio, and notarization requires it.
# EQ_HARDENED_RUNTIME=1

# The oldest macOS to build for. Process taps need 14.2.
# MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=14.2
